    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65615",
      "knowledge_point_id": "5f9683d9-05b9-5a42-9f81-0170a15dfac1",
      "knowledge_point": "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios",
      "problem": "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{64}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={32}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {32}^{∘})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{32}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{64}^{∘}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{32}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{64}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{32}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{32}^{∘}\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{32}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
