      "math_academy_id": "q-24645",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d820aa9f-7c02-53b5-9b09-c25ee59dbdaf",
      "knowledge_point": "Using the Double-Angle Formula Multiple Times",
      "problem": "$4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
