    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-364639",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d820aa9f-7c02-53b5-9b09-c25ee59dbdaf",
      "knowledge_point": "Using the Double-Angle Formula Multiple Times",
      "problem": "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
