    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3619",
      "knowledge_point_id": "5f9683d9-05b9-5a42-9f81-0170a15dfac1",
      "knowledge_point": "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios",
      "problem": "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\sin 25^\\circ}{\\sin 50^\\circ}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\sin{2x} = 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=25^\\circ$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(2\\cdot 25^\\circ\\right) = 2\\sin 25^\\circ \\cos 25^\\circ\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\sin 50^\\circ = 2\\sin 25^\\circ \\cos 25^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the ${\\color{blue} \\sin 50^\\circ}$ in the denominator as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘}} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{25}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}}}{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{25}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{25}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡{25}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3623",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4c898bbf-004c-5905-a0a0-761adefc8bd8",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Exact Value of a Trigonometric Expression Using the Double-Angle Formula",
      "problem": "Without using a calculator, find the exact value of $3\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac {\\pi}{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {8} \\right) \\cos \\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {8} \\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the above by $\\dfrac 1 2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3 \\, {\\color{black}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)} } = 3 \\left( \\color{black} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3618",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d820aa9f-7c02-53b5-9b09-c25ee59dbdaf",
      "knowledge_point": "Using the Double-Angle Formula Multiple Times",
      "problem": "Without using a calculator, find the exact value of $\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(2\\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right) .\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},$ we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
